We start by asking, "what can we multiply by $x+3$ to get $x^2+5x+k$ where $k$ is an integer?"

The answer is $x+2$:

$\blueD{(x+3)(x+2)=x^2+5x+6}$

Now we rewrite the numerator and split the quotient:

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{x^2+5x+5}{x+3}&=\dfrac{(\blueD{x^2+5x+6})-1}{x+3}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\blueD{x^2+5x+6}}{x+3}-\dfrac{1}{x+3}
\end{align}$

Now we use the fact that $\blueD{(x+3)(x+2)=x^2+5x+6}$ to rewrite the numerator of the first quotient and cancel common factors:

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{\blueD{x^2+5x+6}}{x+3}-\dfrac{1}{x+3}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\cancel{\blueD{(x+3)}}\blueD{(x+2)}}{\cancel{x+3}}-\dfrac{1}{x+3}
\\\\
&=x+2-\dfrac{1}{x+3}
\end{align}$
